// The key-value cache a workload reaches, as the vocabulary's `cache` kind.
//
// # Its own package, and why every capability is
//
// A world is what a component targets, and a package is what a world is
// versioned and shipped as. Folding eleven capabilities into one package made
// all eleven one version: adding a field to `storage` would have moved the
// version a cache's guest was built against, for a change that cannot touch it.
//
// It also made the set fixed. tau's `implements:` already names worlds beyond
// the handler — an authorizer, a router, a gateway transformer — and each of
// those serves a DIFFERENT subset of these. A subset is a list of packages, and
// there is nothing to pick from when the package is the whole set.
//
// # The cache is named by its LABEL and not by its id
//
// A workload declares what it reaches in its own configuration:
//
//     cache:
//         sessions:
//             ops: [get, put]
//
// and `sessions` is the string it passes here. The author wrote that label and
// never sees the id it resolves to, which is also what makes the guest portable
// between environments: staging's `sessions` and production's are different
// caches and the same code.
//
// # One that holds many instances is addressed `label/instance`
//
// A cache declaring `instances:` holds many, each admitted by the function the
// target named. A label cannot contain a slash — the vocabulary's own rule —
// so `sessions/room-42` splits with no ambiguity, and an instance is refused
// before the store is touched.
//
// # Every op is checked against that declaration
//
// A label the resource did not declare is `no-such-cache`, and an op it did not
// ask for is `denied` — both before the store is touched. The configuration is
// the grant; there is no second place to widen it.
package taubyte:cache@0.1.0;

interface store {
    // What went wrong, as arms an author can act on differently.
    //
    // `not-found` IS NOT AN ERROR CONDITION and is the common case: a cache
    // miss is what a cache is for. It carries no payload because there is
    // nothing to say beyond the key the caller already has.
    variant error {
        // The label is not in this resource's `cache:` block. A typo, or a
        // grant nobody wrote.
        no-such-cache(string),
        // The label is declared, but not with this op.
        denied(string),
        not-found,
        // One value larger than the whole cache. Evicting everything would
        // still not make it fit.
        too-large,
        // The store could not answer. Distinct from a miss on purpose: a
        // caller retries one and recomputes for the other.
        unavailable(string),
    }

    // get returns a value, or not-found.
    get: func(name: string, key: string) -> result<list<u8>, error>;

    // put writes one. It is an upsert, and it evicts rather than refusing when
    // the cache is at its declared size — losing an entry costs a
    // recomputation, which is the whole premise of this kind.
    put: func(name: string, key: string, value: list<u8>) -> result<_, error>;

    // delete removes one. Deleting what is not there succeeds.
    delete: func(name: string, key: string) -> result<_, error>;

    // list names the live entries under a prefix, sorted. An expired entry is
    // not listed.
    //
    // `%list` ESCAPES A WIT KEYWORD, and the escape is worth it: the verb the
    // configuration writes is `list`, and a function called anything else here
    // would be a second spelling of one verb.
    %list: func(name: string, prefix: string) -> result<list<string>, error>;
}

// A guest that needs only a cache.
world imports {
    import store;
}
